WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.853
This video shows how to create a virtual machine on a Localnet network,

2
00:00:04.853 --> 00:00:10.047
with a fixed MAC address, in the lab for OpenShift Virtualization on IBM Cloud.

3
00:00:10.047 --> 00:00:14.969
The virtual machine gets its IP address from the IBM Cloud VPC subnet.

4
00:00:14.969 --> 00:00:19.741
We start in the IBM Cloud console, and finish in the OpenShift web console.

5
00:00:20.941 --> 00:00:24.738
Before we start, here is the network this virtual machine will use.

6
00:00:24.738 --> 00:00:27.010
It is a Localnet network.

7
00:00:27.010 --> 00:00:31.957
It joins the virtual machine straight onto a subnet of the IBM Cloud VPC.

8
00:00:31.957 --> 00:00:36.704
Each lab has its own Localnet network, in its own project, on its own VLAN.

9
00:00:36.704 --> 00:00:40.633
The cluster tags the virtual machine's traffic with that VLAN,

10
00:00:40.633 --> 00:00:45.926
and a virtual network interface, or VNI, carries it into the lab's VPC subnet.

11
00:00:45.926 --> 00:00:50.599
Each virtual machine needs its own VNI, and the lab team already made yours.

12
00:00:50.599 --> 00:00:53.871
A VNI has a MAC address and an IP address.

13
00:00:53.871 --> 00:00:57.014
We give the virtual machine the same MAC address,

14
00:00:57.014 --> 00:00:59.643
so the VPC gives it the VNI's IP address.

15
00:01:00.843 --> 00:01:03.565
We start in the IBM Cloud console.

16
00:01:03.565 --> 00:01:06.058
We open the menu, choose Infrastructure,

17
00:01:06.058 --> 00:01:09.587
then Network, and click Virtual network interfaces.

18
00:01:09.587 --> 00:01:12.056
A VNI is the VPC resource that gives a

19
00:01:12.056 --> 00:01:15.459
virtual machine its IP address and MAC address.

20
00:01:15.459 --> 00:01:18.781
The list is empty, because the region is Frankfurt.

21
00:01:18.781 --> 00:01:22.128
The lab runs in Dallas, so we change the region to Dallas.

22
00:01:27.777 --> 00:01:30.624
To see what a VNI needs, we click Create.

23
00:01:30.624 --> 00:01:35.146
We do not create anything here, because the lab team already made your VNI.

24
00:01:35.146 --> 00:01:39.286
The form shows an authorization error, and the resource group is empty,

25
00:01:39.286 --> 00:01:41.968
because a lab user may not create a VNI.

26
00:01:41.968 --> 00:01:44.612
We scroll down to the network configuration,

27
00:01:44.612 --> 00:01:47.365
where the VPC and the subnet are chosen.

28
00:01:47.365 --> 00:01:48.737
We go back to the list.

29
00:01:49.939 --> 00:01:52.661
Back in the list, we find our own VNI.

30
00:01:52.661 --> 00:01:59.108
We type its name, lab-4399-ph-lab-39-vm-1, in the search box.

31
00:01:59.108 --> 00:02:00.780
One row is left.

32
00:02:00.780 --> 00:02:05.677
Its Primary IP is 10.26.5.52.

33
00:02:05.677 --> 00:02:08.474
This is the address our virtual machine will get.

34
00:02:09.675 --> 00:02:12.638
The VNI page itself does not open for a lab user,

35
00:02:12.638 --> 00:02:15.347
so we cannot read its MAC address here.

36
00:02:15.347 --> 00:02:18.919
Your lab sheet gives the MAC address and the IP address.

37
00:02:18.919 --> 00:02:25.916
We will use the MAC address from the sheet: 02:00:01:01:9F:46.

38
00:02:27.118 --> 00:02:30.390
We can see how the VNI is attached to the cluster.

39
00:02:30.390 --> 00:02:33.136
We go to OpenShift virtualization,

40
00:02:33.136 --> 00:02:37.687
open virtualization-4399, and click VNI attachments.

41
00:02:37.687 --> 00:02:44.284
The list shows the attachment of our VNI, lab-4399-ph-lab-39-vm-1.

42
00:02:45.485 --> 00:02:49.657
We click the blue OpenShift web console button at the upper right.

43
00:02:49.657 --> 00:02:52.454
The console opens, already signed in.

44
00:02:52.454 --> 00:02:56.776
In the left menu, we open Virtualization, and click VirtualMachines.

45
00:02:56.776 --> 00:03:00.101
We turn off Show only projects with VirtualMachines,

46
00:03:00.101 --> 00:03:02.723
search for lab-39, and click our project.

47
00:03:03.923 --> 00:03:05.395
We click Create.

48
00:03:05.395 --> 00:03:08.192
The wizard opens on Deployment details.

49
00:03:08.192 --> 00:03:11.564
We name the virtual machine nt-localnet-vm.

50
00:03:11.564 --> 00:03:15.586
In Guest OS, we choose Other Linux and centos.stream9.

51
00:03:15.586 --> 00:03:18.883
In Boot source, we select centos-stream9.

52
00:03:18.883 --> 00:03:21.591
In Compute resources, we choose General Purpose,

53
00:03:21.591 --> 00:03:23.905
and change the size from medium to small.

54
00:03:26.075 --> 00:03:28.447
Step 5 is Customization.

55
00:03:28.447 --> 00:03:30.594
We open the Network tab.

56
00:03:30.594 --> 00:03:34.591
On the default interface, we click the kebab menu, and choose Edit.

57
00:03:34.591 --> 00:03:41.363
In the Network list, we choose lab-4399-ph-lab-39, our own Localnet network.

58
00:03:41.363 --> 00:03:45.410
We expand Advanced Settings, and type the MAC address from the sheet.

59
00:03:45.410 --> 00:03:46.357
We click Save.

60
00:03:47.898 --> 00:03:50.295
Next, we set the guest password.

61
00:03:50.295 --> 00:03:54.592
We open the Initial run tab, and click Edit in the Cloud-init section.

62
00:03:54.592 --> 00:03:56.489
The user is centos.

63
00:03:56.489 --> 00:04:00.661
We type the password in the password field, and it shows only as dots.

64
00:04:00.661 --> 00:04:01.933
Then we click Apply.

65
00:04:03.133 --> 00:04:07.455
We click Next to reach Review and create, and then Create VirtualMachine.

66
00:04:07.455 --> 00:04:09.627
The page for our virtual machine opens.

67
00:04:10.827 --> 00:04:14.674
The status changes from Provisioning, to Starting, to Running.

68
00:04:14.674 --> 00:04:16.521
This takes under a minute.

69
00:04:16.521 --> 00:04:19.418
When it runs, we look at the Network tile.

70
00:04:19.418 --> 00:04:23.415
The IP address is the one on our lab sheet, from the VPC subnet.

71
00:04:25.045 --> 00:04:29.317
We click the header of the Network tile, to open the network configuration.

72
00:04:29.317 --> 00:04:33.889
The network is our Localnet network, and the MAC address is the one we typed.

73
00:04:33.889 --> 00:04:35.561
It matches the VNI.

74
00:04:36.763 --> 00:04:40.835
We open the Console tab, and type the user name, centos.

75
00:04:40.835 --> 00:04:42.782
Then we type the password.

76
00:04:42.782 --> 00:04:44.829
It does not show on screen.

77
00:04:44.829 --> 00:04:46.151
We wait for the prompt.

78
00:04:47.351 --> 00:04:49.223
We run ip addr show.

79
00:04:49.223 --> 00:04:54.270
The interface eth0 has the reserved address from the VPC, marked dynamic.

80
00:04:55.471 --> 00:04:58.968
Last, we run curl, with the address of the lab web page.

81
00:04:58.968 --> 00:05:03.315
The page answers, Well done, lab completed, and it names our address.

82
00:05:03.315 --> 00:05:07.337
The virtual machine is on its own lab network, in the VPC subnet.

83
00:05:08.537 --> 00:05:12.838
You created a virtual machine with a fixed MAC address on a Localnet network,

84
00:05:12.838 --> 00:05:17.434
set its password with cloud init, and checked its IP address from the guest.

85
00:05:17.434 --> 00:05:21.506
When you have finished with a virtual machine, delete it, so that it frees its disk.

